[09:16] orchestrator     directive 2 from steer: Stronger form of directive 1: it removes the k = F_n - 1 restriction and is exact in integer arithmetic. Verified outside the container for k = 3,5,8,10,13,17,21,26,34,40,55 against the brute oracle. Model the k+1 factors as a mechanical word: with slope a taken as the RATIONAL F(n-1)/F(n) for any F(n) much larger than k, cut the unit circle by the k+1 points frac(-m*a), m = 0..k, take the midpoint x of each of the k+1 arcs, and set digit j of that factor to floor(x + (j+1)a) - floor(x + j a). Checked: this reproduces the brute factor set exactly at every k above, non-Fibonacci k included, and rational a makes every quantity an exact integer computation. Now Abel-summate the value. With v(x) = sum over j of digit_j * 10^(k-1-j), telescoping gives v(x) = floor(x + k*a) - 10^(k-1)*floor(x) + 9 * sum over j = 1..k-1 of 10^(k-1-j) * floor(x + j*a). Checked exactly for every representative at every k above, and the sum of v(x)^2 over the k+1 representatives reproduces the brute Psi exactly. So Psi(k) is a SECOND MOMENT of a geometrically weighted floor sum, with no factor enumeration, no k -> k+1 stepping, and no Psi-period. The primitive to build is the universal Euclidean algorithm (the monoid generalisation of the AtCoder floor_sum, also called Chtholly's algorithm), carrying the tuple (count, sum of x^j, sum of x^j*floor, sum of x^j*floor^2) mod 101001001 with x = 10^(-1) mod M, which evaluates such a sum in O(log) steps. Build it, check it against a brute oracle on k = 1..150 and against the given Psi(10) = 10699667 mod M, then run it at k = 10^18 with a Fibonacci index large enough that F(n) exceeds k. Note 10 is invertible mod M since M = 101001001 is prime and does not divide 10, so negative powers of 10 are legitimate mod M.
