[128:16] orchestrator     directive 4 from steer: Build the universal-Euclidean primitive NOW, ahead of any further structural probing. Toeplitz defects and extension-recurrence residues are not on the critical path; the only thing between this run and the answer is an O(log) evaluator for a geometrically weighted floor sum and its second moment. Spec: walk the lattice path of y = (p*t+q)/r for t = 1..n, one R step per unit of t, one U step per unit increase of floor(y), and take a monoid product over the path, split by the Euclidean recursion as in the AtCoder floor_sum, so the path costs O(log) merges. A node carries, for its segment: dR, dU, w = z^dR with z the geometric ratio mod M, and S0 = sum of z^t over its R steps, S1 = sum of z^t*floor(y), S2 = sum of z^t*floor(y)^2, all relative to the segment origin, all mod 101001001. Compose left l with right r as: dR = l.dR+r.dR; dU = l.dU+r.dU; w = l.w*r.w; S0 = l.S0 + l.w*r.S0; S1 = l.S1 + l.w*(r.S1 + l.dU*r.S0); S2 = l.S2 + l.w*(r.S2 + 2*l.dU*r.S1 + l.dU^2*r.S0). Identity is zeros with w = 1. Those dU shifts carry floor values across a boundary and are the one place this primitive goes wrong. Acceptance tests, in order, none skipped: (1) S0 vs a direct loop on random p,q,r,n,z; (2) S1 vs plain floor_sum at z=1 and vs a direct loop at z not 1; (3) S2 vs a direct loop; (4) directive 2's telescoped v evaluated through the primitive vs code/mech/mech_psi.py on k=1..150 and vs Psi(10)=10699667; (5) the Phase 4 anchors Psi(10^4)=16242174 and Psi(10^6)=77578256 mod M, reproduced exactly and in negligible time. Only after (5) passes, run k=10^18 with a Fibonacci approximant F(n-1)/F(n) whose F(n) exceeds 10^18, and confirm the answer is stable across two different approximants. If the outer sum over the k+1 representatives resists one pass, note the x_m are themselves the orbit frac(-m*a), so m is another floor-sum index: carry the joint state in the same monoid rather than looping over m.
